# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""Regenerated commentary for Sūrah al-Anbiyāʾ (21), verses 20-31.

Grounded in the note apparatus of initial/021.md (T = al-Tabari, Q = al-Qurtubi,
R = al-Razi, Su = al-Sulami, IK = Ibn Kathir).
"""
SURAH = "al-Anbiyāʾ"
NUM = 21

SECTIONS = {

20: (None, [
 ("Glorification Without Interval", [
  "Verse 19 established that the beings with God are neither arrogant nor weary; verse 20 states what they do instead. *Yusabbiḥūna al-layla wa-l-nahāra lā yafturūn* — \"They glorify night and day, without tiring.\" The pairing of night and day is not decoration. It is the Qur'an's standard idiom for unbroken duration, and it forecloses the natural human assumption that worship must be interrupted by rest, boredom, or distraction. The angels' praise is continuous in a way that no human worship can be.",
  "Al-Ṭabarī records an observation that gives the image its force: for the angels, the glorification of God is what breathing is for human beings. Breathing is not an act one performs between other acts; it is the condition of performing any act at all. The analogy therefore does not merely say that the angels praise God often. It says that praise is their mode of existence, as involuntary and as sustaining as respiration.",
 ]),
 ("Why the Angels Cannot Weary", [
  "Al-Rāzī draws the theological consequence that al-Ṭabarī had left implicit. Not tiring and not becoming bored is possible only for angels, not for human beings. The distinction matters because it prevents the verse from becoming an impossible demand addressed to the reader. Human worship is necessarily intermittent, and the Qur'an does not ask it to be otherwise; what it asks is that the intervals be filled with something other than competing worship.",
  "There is a second implication. Fatigue in worship arises when the object of worship is limited — when one has exhausted what there is to say about it. The angels' indefatigable praise is thus evidence about its object rather than about them: they do not tire because there is no bottom to what they are praising. The commentators connect this to 41:38, where the same refusal of arrogance and weariness is attributed to those near God.",
 ]),
 ("The Contrast the Verse Is Building", [
  "The sūrah has been assembling an argument, and verse 20 is the final piece before it turns. Verses 19–20 describe worship that is continuous, ungrudging, and untiring. Verse 21 then asks a single question — *Or have they taken gods from the earth who resurrect?* — and the force of that question depends entirely on what has just been said. The beings nearest to God worship without ceasing; the gods taken from the earth cannot even give life.",
  "The rhetorical movement is deliberate. The sūrah does not begin by attacking the idols. It first establishes the standard of worship among the highest created beings, so that the worship of stones appears not merely false but absurdly beneath the dignity of the worshipper. Verse 20 supplies the measure against which verse 21's question is asked.",
 ]),
]),

21: (None, [
 ("A Single Question", [
  "*Am ittakhadhū ālihatan mina'l-arḍi hum yunshirūn.* The verse is a question, not an assertion, and its brevity is the point. After nineteen verses establishing God's ownership of the heavens and the earth and describing the ceaseless worship of the angels, the sūrah compresses its whole critique of idolatry into one clause: have they taken gods *from the earth* who *resurrect*?",
  "Two words carry the argument. *From the earth* fixes the origin of these gods — they are not descendents from the heavens, not even fallen ones, but products of the ground. *Who resurrect* fixes the test — the one function that a god must be able to perform and that no idol has ever been observed to perform.",
 ]),
 ("The Argument al-Qurṭubī Draws Out", [
  "Al-Qurṭubī's note is economical: rather than worship the Creator of the heavens and the earth, such people prefer to worship earthly beings incapable of giving life. The word *prefer* is important, because it identifies the choice as a preference rather than a conclusion. Nobody arrives at the worship of a stone by reasoning; people arrive at it by inheritance, by social pressure, or by the convenience of a deity that makes no demands.",
  "The verse's use of *resurrect* rather than a lesser function is a deliberate choice of the hardest case. An idol can be credited with many things by generous observers — presence, blessing, intercession. It cannot be credited with raising the dead. The sūrah therefore does not argue about the marginal claims; it goes straight to the one that decides the matter.",
 ]),
 ("Resurrection as the Qur'an's Recurring Test", [
  "The source commentary notes that biological life renewed is treated throughout the Qur'an as a symbol of the Resurrection, and cites 35:9: God sends the winds, they raise clouds, the clouds are driven to a dead land, and the earth is revived after its death — *thus shall be the Resurrection*. The argument is not that the two events are alike in mechanism but that both display the same impossible transition: from nothing to something, from death to life.",
  "This is why the question is phrased as it is. The Meccan audience already accepted that the earth is revived annually. The verse asks them to apply the observation they already grant to the deity they have chosen. If the revival of a dead land requires the Creator of the heavens and the earth, then a god taken from the earth cannot be the one performing it.",
 ]),
]),

22: (None, [
 ("The Logical Argument", [
  "*Law kāna fīhimā ālihatun illa'Llāh la-fasadatā.* \"Were there gods other than God in them, they would surely have been corrupted.\" This is one of a small group of verses that argue the impossibility of a plurality of gods as a matter of logic rather than revelation. The commentators place it alongside 12:39–40, where Joseph asks his fellow prisoners whether diverse lords are better than God the One, the Paramount, and 23:91, which reasons that if there were other gods, each would take away what it created and some would overcome others.",
  "The argument runs from the observable order of the world to the unity of its source. The heavens and the earth display a single coherent system — one set of physical regularities, no interruption, no negotiation between competing agencies. A plurality of sovereigns would produce exactly the interruption that is not observed. Al-Rāzī develops this as a demonstration rather than a rhetorical flourish, and 17:42 makes the same point from the opposite direction: if there were gods with Him, they would seek a way unto the Possessor of the Throne.",
 ]),
 ("The Grammar That Carries the Formula", [
  "The source commentary notes that *illa'Llāh* can be rendered either \"other than God\" or \"except for God,\" and that the second reading reproduces the second half of *lā ilāha illa'Llāh*. This is not a curiosity. It means the verse's central clause is structurally identical to the declaration of faith, so that the logical argument and the creedal formula are the same sentence in two registers.",
  "The commentary also notes that *fīhimā* — \"in them\" — uses a dual pronoun referring to the heavens and the earth. The duality is significant: the verse takes the two most comprehensive domains of created existence and asserts that neither contains a competing god. There is no third domain in which such a god could be located.",
 ]),
 ("Al-Sulamī's Spiritual Reading", [
  "The note records that al-Sulamī reads the verse as an allegory of the heart: a divided heart will become corrupted, and a heart must be unified and detached from entanglements in the world. On this reading *fa-sadatā* — \"they would surely have been corrupted\" — describes not a cosmological collapse but the disintegration of an interior life divided between competing objects of ultimate concern.",
  "The allegory preserves the logical structure of the verse rather than replacing it. What makes a plurality of gods impossible in the cosmos — that competing sovereigns produce disorder rather than order — is what makes a plurality of ultimates destructive in a person. The commentators do not treat these as two separate meanings but as the same truth operating at two scales.",
 ]),
]),

23: (None, [
 ("Asymmetry of Answerability", [
  "*Lā yus'alu ʿammā yafʿalu wa-hum yus'alūn.* \"He shall not be questioned about what He does, but they shall be questioned.\" Al-Rāzī states the point plainly: God is not answerable to creation, but creatures are answerable to Him. The verse does not argue for this asymmetry; it announces it, and the announcement is itself part of the argument, since a being who could be called to account by his creatures would not be sovereign.",
  "The source commentary notes that the idea of questioning in the sense of responsibility also arises at verse 13 of this sūrah and at 2:119, 2:134, and 16:56. The recurrence matters. The Qur'an returns repeatedly to the theme because it is the point at which divine sovereignty and human moral seriousness meet, and neither can be surrendered without losing the other.",
 ]),
 ("Can One Ask God Why?", [
  "Al-Qurṭubī and al-Rāzī both connect the verse to teleology — whether human beings may ask God why He did such and such. The commentary offers an alternative rendering that sharpens the issue: *He is unanswerable for what He does, but they are answerable*. Under this reading the verse is not forbidding a question but stating a structural fact: with created things one can ask why they acted as they did, because they act from motives and causes; it is by definition impossible for creatures to know why God does what He does.",
  "Al-Rāzī identifies the theological stake precisely. What is at issue is whether God stands in need of a reason outside Himself in order to act. If He did, that reason would be prior to Him and would be the true sovereign. The verse's asymmetry is therefore a consequence of divine unity rather than an assertion of arbitrary power — the same unity argued in the preceding verse from the coherence of the cosmos.",
 ]),
 ("The Question of Predestination", [
  "Al-Qurṭubī records that the issue of free will versus predestination arises here, in that human beings cannot, on a strictly predestinarian reading, question God or ask why one was made a disbeliever in life. The commentary links this to the phrase *God provides for whomsoever He will without reckoning*, understood by some to mean that no one can bring God to account for what He gives or withholds.",
  "The verse does not resolve this tension, and the commentators do not pretend that it does. What it does is locate the tension honestly: creatures are answerable, and the ground of their answerability is not something they can audit. The classical readings differ over how much room remains for human agency, but they agree that the verse forecloses the demand for an account from God while insisting on an account to Him.",
 ]),
]),

24: (None, [
 ("The Demand for Proof", [
  "The verse returns to the question posed at verse 21 — *Or have they taken gods apart from Him?* — and this time it does not leave the question rhetorical. It converts it into a demand: *Say, \"Bring me your proof!\"* The Qur'an's use of *burhān* here is technical. The source commentary notes that *burhān* means a demonstration or evidence, and that the word is used in the same sense at 2:111, 23:117, 27:64, and 28:75.",
  "This is a striking move in a text often characterised as demanding submission. The verse does not say *believe because I say so*; it says *produce your demonstration*. It places the burden of argument on the party making the positive claim — that there are gods besides God — and it identifies that burden as one the claimants have never attempted to discharge.",
 ]),
 ("Two Reminders", [
  "*This is the Reminder for those who are with me, and the Reminder for those before me.* The word in both instances is *dhikr*, which the commentary notes can mean invoke, mention, recall, remembrance, or renown. The double occurrence is deliberate: the Prophet ﷺ is told that his own revelation is continuous with what preceded it, not a departure from it.",
  "Al-Rāzī and al-Ṭabarī record two readings. On the first, *Reminder for those before me* refers to the fact that the Qur'an recounts the lives of peoples who passed away, and would be rendered \"Reminder *of* those before me.\" On the second, it refers to the Torah and the Gospel, whose content — like that of the Qur'an — is that human beings should not take gods apart from Him, a point the source commentary notes is reaffirmed at verse 25. Both readings serve the same purpose: the demand for proof is not novel but is what every messenger has made.",
 ]),
 ("Why They Turn Away", [
  "The verse ends with a diagnosis rather than a conclusion: *Nay, but most of them do not know the truth; so they turn away.* The sequence is important. The turning away is not presented as a considered rejection of evidence but as a consequence of not knowing. The commentators read this as an explanation of the demand for proof in the first clause: one asks for a demonstration from people who have never been asked to provide one, and who have therefore never examined what they hold.",
  "The verse thereby completes the movement begun at verse 21. There the question was asked; here the questioner is instructed to ask it aloud, in public, to the claimants themselves. The sūrah's method throughout is not to assert the absurdity of idolatry but to invite its adherents to state their case, on the confident expectation that no case exists to be stated.",
 ]),
]),

25: (None, [
 ("One Message, Many Messengers", [
  "*And We sent no messenger before thee, save that We revealed unto him, \"Verily, there is no god but I; so worship Me!\"* Al-Ṭabarī's note identifies the verse's precise claim: even though the messengers had different commands regarding what was obligatory and forbidden, they all conveyed the same message of monotheism and devotion to God. The distinction is essential to the verse's logic and is frequently lost. The sūrah is not saying that all religions have the same law; it is saying that all revelation has the same first principle.",
  "Al-Qurṭubī adds that this confirms the self-evident existence and Oneness of God. The phrase *self-evident* does real work here. If tawḥīd required demonstration in each generation, it would be a discovery rather than a reminder; the verse's insistence that every messenger received it as revelation implies that it is being restored rather than invented.",
 ]),
 ("The Grammatical Variant and Its Effect", [
  "The source commentary records that some read part of the verse in the third person: *We sent no messenger before thee, but that it was revealed unto him*. The variant shifts the address from the Prophet ﷺ to a general statement about prophethood, and in doing so removes any suggestion that the second-person address singles him out. On either reading the content is identical; what changes is whether the verse is heard as reassurance to one man or as a description of the office he holds.",
  "Both readings support the argument the sūrah has been making since verse 21. The demand for proof at verse 24 was not a personal challenge but the standing challenge of every messenger, and verse 25 establishes that standing by tracing it to the origin of the office itself.",
 ]),
 ("The Connection to What Follows", [
  "The verse closes one argument and opens another. It answers the claim that the Prophet ﷺ has brought something unprecedented by showing that his message is the oldest there is. It then sets up verse 26, which records a specific departure from that message: *And they say, \"The Compassionate has taken a child.\"* The juxtaposition is the point. Verse 25 states what every messenger was given; verse 26 states what some have said against it.",
  "The source commentary directs the reader to the essay \"The Quranic View of Sacred History and Other Religions\" on the fundamental similarities among the messages and messengers. That framing matters for how the verse should be read: it is not a claim that all religious traditions are equivalent but a claim about what was originally revealed to each, and it therefore leaves open the question of what has been preserved.",
 ]),
]),

26: (None, [
 ("Two Claims Behind One Sentence", [
  "*And they say, \"The Compassionate has taken a child.\" Glory be to Him!* The source commentary identifies two distinct beliefs behind the single accusation. Among the idolatrous Quraysh, the claim was that the angels were daughters of God, as at 37:149–53 and 43:16–17. Among Christians, it was the doctrine of the Divine sonship of Jesus, as at 4:171. The verse addresses both without naming either, because the objection it raises applies to both.",
  "Al-Qurṭubī records a specific occasion: the verse was revealed in connection with the tribe of Khuzāʿah, who considered the angels to be daughters of God and worshipped them in order to obtain their intercession. That detail explains why the verse's rebuttal — *Nay, but they are honored servants* — identifies the angels as servants rather than arguing about genealogy.",
 ]),
 ("Why Begetting Is Impossible", [
  "The commentary records a nuance that is easily missed. Some Muslim theologians do not object to calling God \"Father\" in a narrow metaphorical or symbolic sense; what they reject is the assertion that a Divinely begotten being would differ ontologically from other creatures. The objection is not to the word but to the claim of a different order of being.",
  "The theological ground is the one established at verse 22. If God is one and uncomposed, then begetting would introduce distinction within the Divine and would make the begotten either a creature or a second god. Neither is available. The verse's *Glory be to Him* — *subḥānahu* — is the standard Qur'anic marker that a statement has attributed to God something incompatible with His unity.",
 ]),
 ("The Sectarian Error", [
  "The commentary draws a parallel that broadens the verse's scope. A similar error is to believe that people are children of God in an exclusive and sectarian way, as at 5:18: *And the Jews and the Christians say, \"We are the children of God, and His beloved ones.\" Say, \"Why then does He punish you for your sins?\" Nay, but you are mortals of His creating.* The rebuttal there is not metaphysical but practical: a claim to exclusive Divine favour is tested by whether its holders are exempt from accountability.",
  "Verse 27 then describes what the angels actually are — *They precede Him not in speech, and they act according to His Command* — and the description is the proof of verse 26's claim. A child acts on its own initiative; a servant acts on command. The sūrah settles the question not by argument about nature but by observing behaviour.",
 ]),
]),

27: (None, [
 ("Speech Under Command", [
  "*Lā yasbiqūnahu bi'l-qawli wa-hum bi-amrihi yaʿmalūn.* \"They precede Him not in speech, and they act according to His Command.\" Al-Ṭabarī explains the first clause directly: the angels do not speak except as commanded by God and do not act except according to His Will. The verb *yasbiqūna* — \"they precede\" — is chosen with precision. It does not say they do not speak; it says they do not speak *first*.",
  "The distinction matters for the argument the sūrah is running. A being who never speaks before God speaks is a being with no independent initiative, and therefore no claim to divinity. Verse 26 asserted that the angels are honored servants; verse 27 supplies the observable evidence, and the evidence is temporal order rather than metaphysical status.",
 ]),
 ("The Two Halves of the Verse", [
  "The verse pairs speech and action, and the pairing covers the whole of agency. What a being says and what it does are the two modes in which initiative appears. By denying the angels priority in the first and subordinating them in the second, the verse leaves no residue of independent will. This is what makes the rebuttal of verse 26 complete rather than merely assertive.",
  "It also explains why the Khuzāʿah's practice was incoherent. They worshipped the angels to obtain intercession, which presupposes that the angels can act on their own behalf and on behalf of others. Verse 27 states that they cannot: they act according to His Command. Verse 28 then makes the point explicit with regard to intercession itself.",
 ]),
 ("Al-Sulamī's Inward Modelling", [
  "The commentary records al-Sulamī's spiritual reading: one should inwardly model oneself on the angels, striving neither to speak nor to act except in accordance with the Will of God, adhering to the Sunnah, and being with God constantly. The reading does not soften the verse's theological claim; it makes it available as a discipline.",
  "The move is characteristic of the Sufi exegesis the commentators record elsewhere in this sūrah. A statement about angelic ontology becomes a rule for human conduct, and the rule is exacting: not that one should speak and act *mostly* in accordance with God's Will, but that one should not precede Him in either. What is impossible for human beings to achieve perfectly is set as the direction of effort.",
 ]),
]),

28: (None, [
 ("Knowledge Before and Behind", [
  "*Yaʿlamu mā bayna aydīhim wa-mā khalfahum.* \"He knows that which is before them and that which is behind them.\" Al-Ṭabarī reads the phrase as what will take place in the future and what took place in the past; al-Qurṭubī offers the Hereafter and this world. Both readings make the same point about the angels' position: they are situated within time and within knowledge, not outside it.",
  "The clause is placed first in the verse for a reason. Before the verse addresses intercession, it establishes that God's knowledge encompasses the angels entirely. What follows about intercession is therefore not a restriction imposed from without but a consequence of a knowledge that already contains them.",
 ]),
 ("The Conditions on Intercession", [
  "*Wa-lā yashfaʿūna illā li-mani'rtaḍā.* \"They intercede not, save for one with whom He is content.\" The source commentary directs the reader to the Qur'anic treatment of intercession at 2:48 and 2:255, noting that it is usually framed in terms of whom God allows to intercede on the Day of Judgment. The verse therefore does not deny intercession; it denies that intercession is available at the intercessor's discretion.",
  "Al-Rāzī connects the clause to a live theological dispute. For some commentators, *one with whom He is content* includes anyone who believes *There is no god but God*; for the Muʿtazilites, it is restricted to those who believe and have not committed major sins. The verse does not decide the question, and the commentators record both positions rather than harmonising them. What both accept is that the scope of intercession is fixed by God's contentment and not by the intercessor's favour.",
 ]),
 ("Wariness as the Mark of a Creature", [
  "The verse closes: *They are wary, for fear of Him.* Al-Rāzī draws the inference that settles the argument begun at verse 26. The attributes of fear and wariness — the latter also renderable as anxiety — are not those of a Divinely begotten being but of a creature who knows that God is omniscient and omnipotent.",
  "This is the sūrah's method at its most economical. Rather than argue from the nature of the Divine, it observes the disposition of the angels and reads their nature off it. A son does not stand in anxious fear of his father's knowledge. The beings whom the Khuzāʿah worshipped as daughters of God are described as standing in exactly that posture, and the description is the refutation.",
 ]),
]),

29: (None, [
 ("A Conditional, Not a Report", [
  "*And whosoever among them would say, \"Truly I am a god apart from Him,\" such will We requite with Hell.* The verse is a conditional. The source commentary records a disagreement about how to read it: some commentators take it as a specific reference to Iblīs, while others say that it does not describe something anyone actually said but states what would happen if they did.",
  "Al-Rāzī's reading is the more consequential. On his account the verse is not a historical report but a rule, and its force lies in the fact that no angel has ever made the claim. The verse establishes the penalty in order to make the absence of the crime meaningful: these beings are under the same sanction as every other creature, and their obedience is obedience under sanction.",
 ]),
 ("Why the Penalty Must Be Stated", [
  "The argument of verses 26–28 depends on this verse to be complete. If the angels were incapable of the claim by nature, their obedience would carry no merit and their servitude would be no evidence against their divinity. By stating the penalty, the verse establishes that the claim was available to them and was not made — which is precisely what makes their behaviour probative.",
  "The reference to Iblīs serves the same purpose from the opposite side. Iblīs was among the angels in the Qur'anic account of the prostration, and he did make a claim of superiority. The verse's conditional therefore has an instance, and the instance shows that the penalty is not hypothetical.",
 ]),
 ("\"Thus Do We Requite the Wrongdoers\"", [
  "The closing clause generalises: *Thus do We requite the wrongdoers.* The shift from the specific case to the class is significant. The penalty for claiming divinity is not a special sanction reserved for celestial beings; it is the standard requital for wrongdoing, and the claim to be a god apart from God is presented as wrongdoing in its most complete form.",
  "This connects the verse to the sūrah's larger pattern. From verse 21 onward the argument has proceeded by showing that the gods taken from the earth cannot perform the functions of a god. Verse 29 completes it by showing that even the highest of creatures would be requited for claiming the office. The conclusion the sūrah leaves is not that the angels are unworthy of honour but that honour and divinity are different things, and that confusing them is the error the sūrah has been tracing.",
 ]),
]),

30: (None, [
 ("The Rending Asunder", [
  "*Have those who disbelieve not considered that the heavens and the earth were sewn together and We rent them asunder?* The verse's opening — *a-wa-lam yara* — is an appeal to observation, not to revelation. The sūrah asks its audience to consider something they can see, and then draws a conclusion they have been resisting.",
  "The source commentary records four readings of the rending. Some see it as God's making the earth into seven earths and the heaven into seven heavens, alluded to at 65:12. Others read it as the pulling apart of the heavens and the earth, which were originally connected or adjacent. Al-Qurṭubī offers that they were a single thing which God separated with air and wind. A fourth reading, preferred by some because of the phrase that follows, holds that the heavens are rent asunder by rain and the earth by the vegetation that grows from it.",
 ]),
 ("Abū Muslim al-Iṣfahānī's Reading", [
  "Al-Rāzī preserves an interpretation that differs in kind from the others. For Abū Muslim al-Iṣfahānī, *sewn together* (*ratq*) symbolises the state of things before creation, where nothingness was all of a piece and nothing could be distinguished from anything else; *rent asunder* (*fatq*) symbolises the act of creation itself, in which realities once joined in nonexistence come to be separate as they take on existence and individual identity.",
  "On this reading the verse is not describing a physical event but the structure of creation as such. Individuation *is* the rending. To exist is to be distinguished, and the distinction is not something added to existence but is what existence consists in. The commentators record this alongside the physical readings without ranking it against them.",
 ]),
 ("Water and the Closing Question", [
  "*And We made every living thing from water.* The commentators note that this phrase is one reason the rain-and-vegetation reading of the rending is preferred: the verse moves from separation to life, and water is the link. The claim is universal — *every living thing* — and it is stated without qualification in a desert society for which water was the obvious limit on life.",
  "The verse ends: *Will they not, then, believe?* The *then* is consequential. The sūrah has not offered a proof from authority but an observation from experience, and the question asks why the observation does not produce the conclusion. This is the same method as verse 21, where the audience was asked to consider whether gods taken from the earth can resurrect. In both cases the argument is that the evidence is already in hand and has simply not been applied.",
 ]),
]),

31: (None, [
 ("Mountains as Pegs", [
  "*And We placed firm mountains in the earth, lest it shake beneath them.* The verse presents the mountains as functional rather than decorative: they were placed, they have a purpose, and the purpose is the stability of the ground beneath human beings. The Qur'an returns to this image repeatedly, and its force in a seismically active region is immediate.",
  "The commentators read the verse within the same framework as verse 30. Where verse 30 described the separation of the heavens and the earth, verse 31 describes the settling of the earth after that separation. The sequence is creation as a series of deliberate acts, each addressing a condition that would otherwise make the world uninhabitable.",
 ]),
 ("Wide Tracts as Paths", [
  "*And We made wide tracts between them as paths, that haply they may be guided.* The second half of the verse is often passed over, and it is the more interesting. The mountains are not merely stabilisers; the spaces between them are corridors. The verse describes a landscape that is both fixed and traversable — anchored enough to stand on, open enough to travel through.",
  "The word for wide tracts, *fijāj*, denotes routes between mountains. The verse's concern is practical: the Meccan audience were traders whose livelihood depended on passes through the Hijaz. The sūrah points to the infrastructure of their own commerce as evidence of deliberate design, which is the same rhetorical method as verse 30's appeal to water.",
 ]),
 ("\"That Haply They May Be Guided\"", [
  "The clause *laʿallahum yahtadūn* — \"that haply they may be guided\" — carries a double sense that the verse leaves open. Guidance here can mean wayfinding, the physical navigation of a route; it can also mean the guidance that is the sūrah's central concern. The commentators do not resolve the ambiguity, and the verse gains from both readings being available at once.",
  "The pairing is characteristic of the sūrah's argument from verses 30 onward. Having established that God's sovereignty is visible in the structure of the world, it treats the world as navigable in both senses: a traveller who reads the passes correctly arrives, and a reader who reads the same landscape correctly believes. The physical guidance is the visible case of the spiritual one, and the verse's closing clause holds them together without collapsing either into the other.",
 ]),
]),

}
